Advisory findings:

1. Quote-aware url() parsing (implementation hazard). dist/nerv.css has many `url("data:image/svg+xml,...")` values whose quoted payload contains `)` (from `rgb(255, 255, 255)`) and `http://www.w3.org/2000/svg`. A naive `url\([^)]*\)` regex truncates these, and a naive scan for `http://` or `//` flags them as remote. The generator's remaining-remote scan (E5) and the tests' url extraction (B2, B3, B4 blanking, B5) must tokenize `url()` with quoted strings in mind and classify by the value's prefix (`data:`, scheme, `//`, relative). Otherwise B2 or E5 fail falsely, or B4 compares mangled text. Put one shared extractor in the test file, or have the tests consume an exported helper only if it is not the code under test for that assertion.

2. The DSEG7 face has no `unicode-range` (26 faces, 25 ranges). The plan already resolves DSEG7 by parsing its jsDelivr URL, so this works. The implementer must not assume every primary face has a range, and E3 must apply only to gstatic-style faces.

3. The release-ordering mitigation overstates its protection. The plan says the bundle "cannot leave a half-published release". release-please creates the tag and GitHub Release before `publish-npm` runs, so a bundle failure blocks npm publish and the asset upload but leaves a GitHub Release with no npm version and no assets. `npm run build` already has the same risk, so this is not a regression. The real protection is catching font-URL drift before merge. The plan already notes that `npm test` is not in PR CI. The operator may want `npm run build:offline` added to the reusable docs build (cheap, no network beyond npm ci) so CSS font changes fail on the PR, not on release.

4. The pre-mortem's top risk (the zip renders wrong offline although tests pass) is left to a manual operator check. See the innovation item below for a way to make that check trivial.

Radical innovation (advisory, not applied): ship a generated `specimen.html` in the zip root. It links `nerv.css` and `nerv.js` relatively and, for every `@font-face` the generator resolved, renders a line in that family, weight, and style using sample characters drawn from the face's own `unicode-range` (for example the first code points of each subset, including the Shippori kana/CJK slices, DSEG7 digits, and VT323). The generator already has every face, file, and range in hand, so this is roughly 30 lines of templating. No images, all `.nerv-` classes, AGPL like the rest. It turns the manual offline QA into "unzip, turn off network, open specimen.html". It gives consumers a working usage example, and it could later feed an automated headless `document.fonts.check()` smoke test if the project ever adds a browser runner. Cost: one more generated entry, which B5 and B7 already cover generically (manifest hash and no orphans), plus one line in the README section.
